#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Tests for the landing gate: the selector (scripts/lib/land_select.py and
its lexer), the cache key (scripts/land_gate.py) and the host scaler
(scripts/lib/host_budget.py) — and the MUTANTS each test exists to kill.

    python3 scripts/tests/land_gate_test.py            # the tests
    python3 scripts/tests/land_gate_test.py --mutants  # every mutant must turn the suite red
    python3 scripts/tests/land_gate_test.py CoverageLegTests  # only this class

The selector is exercised over a SYNTHETIC repository built in a temp dir, so
every expectation is fixed by this file: a two-workspace Cargo tree with
compiler crates, a std, a compiletest, crates/ members (a core dependency of
the compiler, a leaf, a dev-dependent of the leaf, a crate that walks above
itself to read docs/, an unresolvable reader, a build script that reads
docs/), ui/trust fixtures, run-make dirs, prose, scripts/ and an unmapped path.

A mutant is a change to the gate that would WEAKEN it: a selector that drops a
check the change affects, a cache keyed on fewer inputs, a scaler that
oversubscribes, a classifier that maps an unknown path to less than
everything. `--mutants` applies each in turn and requires the suite to fail;
a mutant that survives is a test that is missing.

Author: Andrew Yates <andrewyates.name@gmail.com>
Copyright 2026 Andrew Yates | License: Apache-2.0 OR MIT
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import os
import shutil
